        "A **neural map** is an ordered representation in which neighboring tissue locations stand for neighboring places in space. In the barn owl, the optic tectum holds an auditory map and a visual map that normally assign a sound and a visible object to the same location. That shared arrangement is the baseline these experiments alter.",
        "Brainard and Knudsen raised juvenile owls with prisms that displaced vision 23° horizontally. Their auditory neurons came to prefer timing values suited to the displaced visual field. Visual receptive fields were then measured without prisms, which fixed the normal anatomical reference for each recording site in the tectum. The neurons changed which timing values they preferred most.",
        "The adaptation changed how acoustic cues corresponded to represented space. Sound sources did not need to move, and sounds did not need to pass through the prisms. The change is sensory calibration rather than a direct optical effect on the sound itself, and the correspondence between cue and location shifted. The measured correspondence is checked again at each site.",
